    "blocks": [
      {"id":"title","type":"markdown","layout":"full","body":"# Parley Size-Eight Confirmation — Iteration 020"},
      {"id":"summary","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Technical summary\n\n**The preregistered parity gate failed 0/4 conditions.** Parley completed 79/80 hidden tasks and 74/80 first checks, but its median was **8.25k reported tokens per task** versus Python's 5.81k and Rust's 6.05k. Median elapsed time was 7.55 seconds per task versus 4.81 and 6.89. The evidence-backed `modulo` alias removed the deterministic rotation parse failure, but overall parity was not reached."},
      {"id":"metrics","type":"metric-strip","layout":"full","cardIds":["sessions_card","tasks_card","gate_card","hidden_card","first_card","multiple_card"]},
      {"id":"scope","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Frozen confirmation scope\n\nTen complete size-eight workload replicates were run for Parley 0.3.153, Python, and Rust: 30 fresh sessions and 240 task assignments. The protocol, task order, hidden tests, fixed instruction, model, and four-condition gate were committed before output. Every session had a unique thread ID, used the exact checker, and passed protocol-integrity validation."},
      {"id":"effort_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Repairs create two Parley regimes\n\nFive Parley bundles were repair-free and clustered near 6.1k tokens per task. Five repaired at least once and ranged from 10.3k to 15.2k. Python and Rust required no repair turns in any bundle. This split explains why a single aggregate obscures the central result: Parley is close when it lands on canonical source immediately, but its surface remains less reliable for this model."},
      {"id":"session_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"session_tokens_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"reliability_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Reliability missed both baseline marks\n\nPython and Rust passed 80/80 tasks on the first check and hidden judgment. Parley passed 74/80 first checks and 79/80 hidden judgments. The hidden miss was a bounded rotation algorithm that handled large shifts incorrectly; it was not a compiler failure. First-check failures were concentrated in deduplication, prefix, and rotation."},
      {"id":"reliability_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"reliability_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"time_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Elapsed time also missed the gate\n\nParley's 7.55-second median per task was 56.8% slower than Python and 9.6% slower than Rust. The preregistered condition required matching the better baseline, so the time condition failed independently of token count and reliability."},
      {"id":"time_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"time_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"clean_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Clean sensitivity: near Rust, still behind Python\n\nThe five repair-free Parley bundles had a 6.13k median tokens per task—**1.37% above Rust and 5.57% above Python**—with 40/40 first checks and 39/40 hidden judgments. This is useful diagnosis, not the primary result: selecting only clean sessions is post hoc and does not satisfy the frozen gate."},
      {"id":"clean_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"clean_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"task_text","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Failure audit and compiler policy\n\nSix first checks failed. `repeat while` recurred across two tasks, but adding it would duplicate canonical `while` and weaken a small, coherent surface, so it is rejected. The three containment phrasings were confined to one task family and are also rejected. One accepted program failed in the Rust backend because a range-loop variable was immutable; that is a general compiler-totality defect and qualifies for correction regardless of benchmark impact."},
      {"id":"task_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"task_failure_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"repair_chart_block","type":"chart","chartId":"repair_chart","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"task_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"task_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"failure_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"failure_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"language_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"language_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"session_table_block","type":"table","tableId":"session_table","layout":"full"},
      {"id":"methodology","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Frozen methodology\n\n- **Matrix:** 30 fresh sessions, ten replicates, three languages, eight tasks per session, seed `20260805`.\n- **Toolchain:** Parley 0.3.153 at `736a474`, `gpt-5.6-sol` medium, Codex CLI 0.146.0.\n- **Instruction:** unchanged 1,519-character skill, SHA `6ca098e4…`; the one allowed compression experiment remains closed.\n- **Protocol:** no workspace inspection; first action writes all solutions; exact `./check` only; hidden cases withheld.\n- **Integrity:** 30 unique thread IDs, 30/30 checker integrity and protocol compliance, no timeouts, runner errors, or nonzero agent exits.\n- **Hashes:** protocol `31da622d…`; frozen result `842b3408…`."},
      {"id":"limitations","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Limitations and uncertainty\n\nThis is a confirmation on one eight-task corpus, one model, one prompt style, and one local machine. Reported tokens are Codex session accounting, not language source length. Wall time includes agent behavior and checker execution. The clean sensitivity subset is selected after observing repairs. The unchanged Parley context is 212 characters per task larger than Python's, but this study cannot assign the residual gap to context alone."},
      {"id":"decision","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Decision and next step\n\n1. Preserve iteration 020 as a failed confirmation; do not claim Python/Rust parity.\n2. Fix only the mutable range-loop backend defect, because accepted programs must compile and the change is generally useful.\n3. Add no `repeat while` or containment aliases from this transcript.\n4. Keep the fixed instruction unchanged and run no second compression experiment.\n5. After the correctness fix, move to a genuinely new broad corpus or model split. Do not tune against these eight tasks again unless a separately justified language change needs regression confirmation."},
      {"id":"questions","type":"markdown","layout":"full","sourceId":"confirmation_results","body":"## Further questions\n\n- Does range-loop mutability compile consistently while preserving bounded-loop semantics?\n- On a new corpus, does Parley's repair-free regime remain within roughly 1–6% of the baselines?\n- Which residual failures recur across unrelated tasks and models strongly enough to justify a language-design review?"}
    ]
  },
